Expo Multi-App Monorepo Architecture Proposal

Overview

This document proposes a production-ready architecture for managing 50+ Expo apps in a single monorepo. The goals are to:

- Maintain individual app identity (bundle IDs, icons, configurations).
- Centralize dependency management (single source of truth).
- Eliminate code duplication (shared components and business logic).
- Make updates scalable (SDK upgrades in hours instead of weeks).
- Work seamlessly with EAS Build and EAS Submit, including Expo’s GitHub UI.
- Reduce storage usage and CI build times via shared dependencies and build caching.

1. Refactoring Approach & Repository Structure

1.1 High-Level Architecture

The recommended architecture is a monorepo structured as follows:

- apps/* – one folder per app, responsible only for app identity and configuration.
- packages/core – shared runtime logic, UI, and business rules.
- packages/expo-preset – centralized Expo/React Native dependency versions.
- pnpm workspaces and Turborepo drive dependency sharing and build orchestration.

1.2 Proposed Repository Layout

expo-multi-app-monorepo/
  apps/                          # One directory per app
    20lifestyle/
      app.json                   # App-specific Expo config
      eas.json                   # App-specific EAS config
      App.tsx                    # Thin wrapper around shared core
      assets/                    # Icons, splash, branded images
      package.json               # Minimal, depends on shared packages
    boundless-movement/
    sorella-online/
    ... (rest of apps)

  packages/
    core/                        # Shared app logic & components
      src/
        index.tsx
        createApp.tsx
        Loader.tsx
        shared.ts
        hooks/...
        features/...(iap, audio, notifications, webview, etc.)
      package.json
    expo-preset/                 # Shared Expo/RN versions
      package.json

  package.json                   # Root scripts & toolchain
  pnpm-workspace.yaml            # Workspace definition (apps/*, packages/*)
  turbo.json                     # Turborepo pipeline (build, lint, test)
  .github/workflows/             # CI + EAS automation (optional)

1.3 Per-App Structure & Identity

Each app under apps/* retains its own identity:

- app.json – name, slug, bundle IDs, icons, splash, and expo.extra values.
- eas.json – EAS build/submit configuration and project linkage.
- assets/ – app-specific icons, splash screens, and branding assets.
- App.tsx – a thin wrapper delegating to shared logic.

Example App.tsx pattern:

import Constants from 'expo-constants';
import { createApp } from '@shared/core';

const extra = (Constants.expoConfig?.extra ?? {}) as any;

export default createApp({
  webViewUrl: extra.webViewUrl ?? 'https://app-slug.mvt.so/',
  appKey: extra.appKey ?? 'app-slug'
  // Additional per-app settings and feature flags can be passed here
});

All substantial logic lives in packages/core, while each app controls only its identity and configuration.

1.4 Handling the 5% App-Specific Logic

Approximately 95% of logic is shared through packages/core. The remaining 5% of app-specific behavior is expressed via:

- Configuration values:
  - URLs, app keys, feature flags, colors, and similar values are stored in app.json → expo.extra.
  - Values are read via expo-constants and passed into createApp.

- Optional flags and callbacks:
  - The AppConfig type in packages/core can define optional fields, for example:
    - enableBonusScreen?: boolean
    - themeVariant?: 'light' | 'dark' | 'brandX'
    - onSpecialEvent?: () => void
  - Selected apps pass these options from their App.tsx wrapper.

- Rare, truly unique components:
  - If an app needs a special component, it can reside in apps/{app}/src.
  - The wrapper can pass it into createApp (for example, specialScreen, customHeader).

This approach keeps shared code generic and extensible, while app-specific behavior is localized, explicit, and typed.

2. Tooling & Infrastructure

2.1 Monorepo Tools

The monorepo is built on:

- pnpm workspaces:
  - Single lockfile and shared dependency store.
  - Efficient installs and strict dependency resolution.
- Turborepo:
  - Task orchestration for build, lint, and test across apps and packages.
  - Build caching and parallelization for faster CI and local workflows.
- TypeScript / ESLint / Prettier:
  - Configured at the root and shared across the monorepo for consistent quality and developer experience.

2.2 Dependency Management Strategy

Expo, React Native, and other shared dependencies are centralized in packages/expo-preset:

{
  "name": "@shared/expo-preset",
  "version": "53.0.0",
  "dependencies": {
    "expo": "~53.0.0",
    "react-native": "0.77.x",
    "react": "18.3.1",
    "expo-device": "~x.y.z",
    "expo-linking": "~x.y.z",
    "expo-notifications": "^...",
    "expo-status-bar": "~3.0.8",
    "react-native-webview": "...",
    "expo-build-properties": "~..."
  }
}

Per-app package.json files stay minimal and reference shared packages:

{
  "name": "app-slug",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "main": "index.ts",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "expo start",
    "android": "expo start --android",
    "ios": "expo start --ios",
    "web": "expo start --web"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@shared/core": "workspace:*",
    "@shared/expo-preset": "workspace:*"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/react": "~19.1.0",
    "typescript": "~5.9.2"
  },
  "private": true
}

This produces a single source of truth for Expo/RN versions, no per-app pinning of core dependencies, and a straightforward upgrade path for the entire fleet.

2.3 pnpm Workspace Configuration

pnpm-workspace.yaml:

packages:
  - 'apps/*'
  - 'packages/*'

The workspace configuration ensures that:

- All apps and packages are part of the same dependency graph.
- workspace:* references resolve correctly.
- A single pnpm install prepares development across the entire monorepo.

2.4 Turborepo Configuration

turbo.json:

{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "pipeline": {
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", ".expo/**"]
    },
    "lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^lint"]
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^test"]
    }
  }
}

Example commands:

- Build a single app:

  pnpm --filter apps/20lifestyle build

- Build all apps:

  turbo run build --filter='apps/*'

Turborepo reuses cached results when code has not changed, reducing CI and local build times significantly.

2.5 Rationale and Trade-offs

Advantages:

- Centralized dependencies and configuration.
- Thin app shells with minimal duplication.
- Efficient disk usage and install times via pnpm.
- Faster and more predictable builds via Turborepo.
- Clear separation between shared logic and per-app identity.

Trade-offs:

- Requires adoption of monorepo practices by the team.
- Demands careful initial extraction of shared logic into packages/core.
- Relies on consistent patterns across apps for maximum benefit.

Overall, this tooling stack balances simplicity, performance, and scalability for a large number of similar apps.

3. EAS Integration

3.1 EAS Build and Submit

Each app retains its own eas.json, for example:

{
  "cli": {
    "version": ">= 16.27.0",
    "appVersionSource": "remote"
  },
  "build": {
    "preview": {
      "env": {
        "EXPO_USE_METRO_WORKSPACE_ROOT": "1"
      },
      "distribution": "internal",
      "credentialsSource": "remote",
      "android": {
        "buildType": "apk"
      }
    },
    "production": {
      "env": {
        "EXPO_USE_METRO_WORKSPACE_ROOT": "1"
      },
      "credentialsSource": "remote",
      "android": {
        "buildType": "app-bundle"
      },
      "autoIncrement": true
    }
  },
  "submit": {
    "production": {}
  }
}

Key aspects:

- EXPO_USE_METRO_WORKSPACE_ROOT=1 allows the EAS build environment to resolve modules from the monorepo root (for example, @shared/core).
- Each app is linked to its own EAS project, preserving independent build and submit histories, separate bundle identifiers and package names, and per-app versioning and release workflows.

Example build and submit commands per app:

cd apps/20lifestyle
eas build --platform android --profile production
eas submit --platform android --profile production

3.2 Expo GitHub UI Integration

In the Expo dashboard and GitHub integration:

- The GitHub repository hosts all apps.
- The Project root for each app is configured to its specific subdirectory, for example apps/20lifestyle.
- Expo reads app.json and eas.json from that subdirectory and treats it as a standalone project.

This preserves independent, UI-triggered builds per app, clear organization of projects and build logs in the Expo dashboard, and compatibility with both CLI and GitHub-based workflows.

3.3 Optional CI Integration

A GitHub Actions workflow can orchestrate EAS builds for multiple apps:

name: EAS Build

on:
  push:
    paths:
      - 'apps/**'
      - 'packages/**'

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    strategy:
      matrix:
        app: [20lifestyle, boundless-movement, sorella-online]
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v3

      - uses: pnpm/action-setup@v2
        with:
          version: 8

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: pnpm install

      - uses: expo/expo-github-action@v8
        with:
          eas-version: latest

      - name: EAS build
        run: eas build --platform android --profile preview --non-interactive
        working-directory: apps/${{ matrix.app }}

Additional apps can be added to the matrix as the fleet grows.

4. Practical Example: Expo SDK Upgrade (52 → 53)

4.1 Scope of Changes

In this architecture, upgrading all apps from Expo SDK 52 to 53 mainly involves:

- Updating shared dependency versions in packages/expo-preset/package.json.
- Adjusting shared code in packages/core to accommodate API or behavior changes.
- Optionally updating shared configurations if required by the official migration guide.

Per-app package.json files do not need manual version bumps, as they consume versions from @shared/expo-preset.

4.2 Upgrade Procedure

1. Update the central preset:

   cd packages/expo-preset
   pnpm add expo@~53.0.0 react-native@0.77.x react@18.3.1 --save
   pnpm update

2. Adjust shared core code:

   cd packages/core
   pnpm install
   # Update code according to the Expo SDK 53 migration guide
   pnpm test

3. Verify with a small number of apps:

   cd apps/20lifestyle
   pnpm install
   expo start --android
   # Perform manual smoke testing of key flows

   Additional representative apps (with slightly different configurations) can be tested similarly to validate coverage.

4. Roll out to all apps:

   cd <repo-root>
   pnpm install
   turbo run build --filter='apps/*'

5. Run preview builds in EAS:

   for app in 20lifestyle boundless-movement sorella-online
   do
     (cd apps/$app && eas build --platform android --profile preview --non-interactive)
   done

4.3 Time and Verification

- Central dependency update and shared core adjustments: typically on the order of 1–2 hours.
- Smoke testing on several apps: 30–60 minutes.
- EAS preview builds: automated, can run in parallel.

Verification combines:

- Automated tests in packages/core (unit and integration tests).
- Typechecking and linting across the monorepo.
- Manual smoke tests for representative apps spanning different configuration patterns.

5. Migration Plan

5.1 Strategy

The migration from separate apps to this monorepo structure can proceed in phases:

1. Foundation:
   - Implement the monorepo structure described above.
   - Ensure packages/core, packages/expo-preset, pnpm-workspace.yaml, and turbo.json are in place.
   - Validate the full workflow for a small number of apps (build, run, EAS build & submit).

2. Batch Migration:
   - Migrate apps in batches, for example 5–10 at a time.
   - After each batch:
     - Run local builds and tests for that batch.
     - Run EAS preview builds.
     - Improve shared logic in packages/core to handle edge cases encountered.

3. Stabilization and Cleanup:
   - Remove old duplicated code once apps are fully migrated.
   - Normalize configuration patterns and align all apps to a shared template where possible.
   - Update internal documentation to reflect the new layout and workflows.

5.2 Per-App Migration Checklist

For each existing app:

- Create the directory apps/app-slug/.
- Move or create:
  - app.json with:
    - expo.name, expo.slug.
    - ios.bundleIdentifier, android.package.
    - icon and splash paths under assets/.
    - expo.extra fields such as webViewUrl, appKey, feature flags, etc.
  - eas.json with standardized build profiles and EXPO_USE_METRO_WORKSPACE_ROOT.
  - assets/ with icons and splash screens.
  - App.tsx thin wrapper that imports createApp from @shared/core and passes in configuration.

- Simplify package.json to depend on @shared/core and @shared/expo-preset via workspace:*.
- Link or confirm EAS project configuration from inside apps/app-slug.
- Validate the app by running expo start locally and an EAS preview build.

5.3 Risks and Mitigation

- Differences in configurations across existing apps:
  - Some apps may use unique plugins or config shapes.
  - Mitigation: migrate a variety of app types early, adapt packages/core and shared presets to cover those scenarios.

- Differences in existing EAS project setups:
  - Some projects may require careful mapping between old and new paths.
  - Mitigation: maintain a mapping of app slugs to EAS project IDs and adjust using eas project:link or eas project:init.

- Team learning curve:
  - The monorepo introduces new patterns.
  - Mitigation: concise internal documentation, code examples, and a migration checklist.

6. Real-World Automation and App Creation

6.1 Automated App Creation Script

A script such as scripts/new-app.sh can automate new app creation:

- Inputs:
  - App slug, display name, iOS bundle ID, Android package, base URL, etc.
- Actions:
  - Create apps/{slug}.
  - Generate:
    - App.tsx using a template that calls createApp.
    - app.json with the appropriate identity and expo.extra values.
    - eas.json with consistent profiles and environment variables.
    - assets/ with default or brand-specific icons and splash images.
  - Optionally invoke EAS setup and additional scripts for assets or metadata.

The script reduces human error and ensures that all new apps follow the same structure and patterns.

6.2 Templates and Supporting Scripts

- Hygen templates can be used to generate:
  - Minimal App.tsx wrappers.
  - app.json and eas.json populated from parameters passed to the script.

- Ruby or other helper scripts can:
  - Process icons and splash screens.
  - Update configurations and metadata within app.json and eas.json.

These tools operate within the apps/{slug} directories and do not need to duplicate shared application logic.

6.3 New App Creation Flow

Once the system is in place, creating a new app follows a simple process:

1. Run:

   ./scripts/new-app.sh app-slug "Display Name" com.example.appslug com.example.appslug

2. The script creates the shell app in apps/app-slug including configuration and assets.

3. The app can immediately be started and built:

   cd apps/app-slug
   pnpm install   # if needed, or rely on root install
   expo start

The new app shares all core logic, ensuring consistent behavior and rapid provisioning.

7. Summary

This monorepo architecture:

- Preserves independent app identity (configs, bundle IDs, branding).
- Centralizes dependencies and shared logic in packages/core and packages/expo-preset.
- Eliminates nearly all code duplication, reducing maintenance overhead.
- Makes Expo SDK upgrades and dependency updates controlled, predictable, and fast.
- Integrates seamlessly with EAS Build, EAS Submit, and Expo’s GitHub UI.
- Improves storage efficiency and CI performance via shared dependencies and build caching.

By consolidating logic and dependencies while keeping app-specific identity at the edge, this structure enables scalable management of a large fleet of Expo apps with clear, maintainable patterns.



